artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider an idealized model of infinite "α matter": an infinite, homogeneous system of nucleons (protons and neutrons in equal numbers, i.e. symmetric nuclear matter) at low baryon density, in which the nucleons are clustered into freely moving α particles (4-nucleon bound states with the quantum numbers of ⁴He). Treat the system fully microscopically, starting from realistic nucleon–nucleon (and, if desired, three-nucleon) interactions, and require that the many-nucleon wave function be fully antisymmetric under exchange of any two nucleons (so that antisymmetrization between nucleons belonging to different α clusters is properly included).

Carry out a first-principles many-body calculation of this system and determine, as functions of the nucleon number density ρ in the low-density regime:

(a) the ground-state energy per nucleon E(ρ)/A of symmetric matter built from α clusters;

(b) the α–α pair distribution function g_{αα}(r), defined so that ρ_α g_{αα}(r) is the conditional density of α-particle centers of mass at separation r given an α at the origin, where ρ_α = ρ/4;

(c) the in-medium α-particle elastic form factor F_α(q;ρ), i.e. the Fourier transform of the intrinsic four-nucleon density distribution of an α cluster embedded in the medium, and in particular its modification relative to the free-space ⁴He form factor as ρ increases.

Provide a calculational scheme that yields these three quantities consistently within a single antisymmetrized many-body framework.

